"rating_margin": 6.06, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 6.39, "baseline_margin": 5.84, "analyst_adjustment": -0.34, "key_factors": ["All nine expert raters put the race on the Democratic side, mostly Lean D", "Prediction markets price Bennett near 84-85% to win", "Kean's incumbency and $3.6M cash on hand keep him within range", "Favorable national midterm environment for Democrats in a zero-PVI district"], "rationale": "The consensus of raters, a single recent poll showing Bennett up five, and markets pricing her near 84% all point to a Democratic edge of roughly five to six points. Kean's incumbency advantage and fundraising keep this from being a Likely race, but the weight of evidence favors a Bennett flip.", "watch": "Sunday night's debate and any late outside spending could tighten the race if Kean lands a blow or Bennett's SALT position becomes a sustained attack.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Thomas Kean Jr. (R) is running."]}, "overview": "New Jersey's 7th District is a pure tossup on paper with an even partisan lean, but first-term Republican incumbent Thomas Kean Jr. is running behind that baseline. Challenger Rebecca Bennett has raised $4.3 million and holds a modest cash disadvantage, while outside groups have spent more to help her than to help Kean. A September poll showed Bennett ahead 47-42 among likely voters.\n\nEvery major race rater now places the district on the Democratic side, ranging from Tilt D to Likely D, and prediction markets give Bennett roughly an 84% chance to win. Kean retains incumbency perks and Chamber of Commerce backing, but the national midterm environment and the weight of expert opinion favor a Democratic flip by a moderate margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8072, "margin": 5.29}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8017, "margin": 5.2}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8142, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8286, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.794, "ma